# LF everywhere, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Without this, a machine with core.autocrlf=true — the Git for Windows default,
# and what this one has — checks every text file out as CRLF while the files
# committed here are LF. Nothing breaks, but git announces the mismatch on every
# file at every `add`, which buried real output under fifty lines of warning.
#
# eol=lf rather than `text=auto` alone: this repo is Node and its output is a
# static site, both of which are LF by convention, and pinning it means the
# checkout is identical on Windows, WSL and the Linux runner that deploys it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Data, not source: diffing a 110 KB generated economy file line by line helps
# nobody. Marked -diff only. NOT -text: excluding a file from normalisation
# while the index already holds it normalised leaves the working copy and the
# index disagreeing about the same bytes, which surfaces later as a file that
# is modified without anyone touching it.
site/data/*.json -diff
vendor/ee2-stats.ndjson -diff
